INT. AGING CELLAR - CHEESE CAVE - NIGHT

A limestone tunnel hung with wheels of cheese on pine shelves, rows of pale moons receding into the dark. Cold, wet air. The smell of ammonia and old milk. A red headlamp sweeps the walls.

She works alone. GRETA SOLBERG, 40s, compact, grey braid tucked into her collar, a stethoscope looped around her neck and a canvas roll of tools at her hip. She runs her palm along the wall, ear tilted to a hidden hum.

She stops at a barrel marked in chalk. Taps it three times. Taps again, lower.

Behind the barrel: a steel door. Wall-sunk. Painted the same grey as the limestone, but no stone ever gleamed like that.

She sets the stethoscope against the lock. Closes her eyes. Turns the dial a hair. Another. Her mouth moves, counting.

The headlamp flickers. She freezes.

Footsteps. Slow. From the entrance of the cave.

She kills the lamp. Dark. She presses herself behind the barrel, a hand sliding to the pry bar at her belt.

A flashlight beam wanders down the tunnel. Along the shelves. The beam bounces off the cheese rinds, ghost-white.

PASCAL ANTOINE, 30s, climbs into frame. Long coat. Soft hat. A paper bag in one hand, a thermos in the other. He hums, off-key. He's not here for her. He's here for the night shift.

He stops. Sniffs the air.

PASCAL
(muttering)
Smells like feet again.

He sets his thermos on a shelf. Eats something from the bag. The hum resumes.

She waits, perfectly still. He turns his back and starts to inspect a wheel, tilting it to the light.

She slides out from behind the barrel. One step. Two. Her boots make no sound on the damp stone.

He stops humming. Doesn't turn.

PASCAL (CONT'D)
(softly)
You're good.

GRETA
You're early.

PASCAL
You're late.

He turns, smiling. She lowers the bar. They regard each other for a long moment across the cheese.

PASCAL (CONT'D)
Did you get it open?

GRETA
Two numbers left.

PASCAL
Then let me hold the light.

He flicks on his flashlight, angles it just so. She kneels at the dial. Her hands steady. The lock turns, a soft double click.

The door swings open on darkness and a faint chill of something better than cheese.
